WEBVTT

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:30.320
<v Ira Glass>OK. As adults, it's pretty rare to have a moment where you're talking with somebody in your own country, in your own hometown, in your own language, and you have no idea what is actually happening, where things become confusing and not in that "oh, what did he mean by that?" sort of way, but in an I Love Lucy, "how did I get myself into this situation?" kind of confusion.
<v Ira Glass>好的。作为成年人，我们很少会遇到这样的时刻：你在自己的国家、自己的家乡、用自己的母语和别人交谈，却完全不知道究竟发生了什么；那种困惑不是“哦，他那句话是什么意思？”的那种，而是一种像《我爱露西》里那种“我是怎么把自己搞到这种境地的？”的困惑。

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:51.840
<v Ira Glass>Like, for example, Amy was back home in Indiana on a break from college. And she was accompanying her brother's high school class on a trip to this health and science museum they have in South Bend called the Healthworks Kids' Museum. And just to give you a sense of who the characters are in this story, Amy's brother is autistic, and he is developmentally disabled.
<v Ira Glass>比如，艾米大学放假回到印第安纳的老家。她陪她弟弟的高中班级去南湾参观一个叫“健康工场儿童博物馆”的健康科学博物馆。为了让你了解这个故事里的人物，艾米的弟弟患有自闭症，且有发育障碍。

00:00:51.840 --> 00:01:03.540
<v Amy>If you had to picture Ben, I would say picture this really handsome, star athlete-looking guy, who sort of has an age of maybe 5 to 8 years old and loves dinosaurs.
<v Amy>如果要你想象本（Ben）的样子，我会说想象一个长得非常帅、看起来像明星运动员的小伙子，他的心智大概只有5到8岁，而且特别喜欢恐龙。

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:23.660
<v Ira Glass>As for Amy, aside from the fact that she's very close to her brother, you can get a good sense of who she is by the conversation that she had with Ben's teacher on the bus with all the developmentally disabled kids driving to this museum. Amy was talking to the teacher about her schoolwork, which, frankly, seems very, very hard. Amy was majoring in physics and studying in Germany.
<v Ira Glass>至于艾米，除了她和弟弟关系非常亲密之外，你可以通过她在公共汽车上与本的老师交谈来了解她，当时车上载着所有去参观博物馆的发育障碍儿童。艾米在和老师谈论她的学业，老实说，这看起来非常非常难。艾米主修物理，还在德国留学。

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:32.980
<v Amy>In Germany, not only were the classes in German, but we were also using all of these graduate textbooks, and so. But she was really nice, and she just listened to me the whole way over.
<v Amy>在德国，不仅课程是用德语上的，我们还用了所有这些研究生级别的教科书，所以……但她人真的很好，她全程都在听我说话。

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:39.480
<v Ira Glass>So they get to the museum. And after some videos and some science experiments where they blow things up, it was time to walk around the exhibits.
<v Ira Glass>于是他们到了博物馆。在看完一些视频和做了一些爆炸科学实验后，是时候去参观各个展区了。

00:01:39.480 --> 00:02:04.410
<v Amy>So during all of this, one of the things that they had been really pushing, they had these new computers that were installed. They were computers designed to walk kids through making a really neat identification card. And they were all really excited about this. I think they must have just gone live, like, the day before we came because they were all just-- make sure you use our ID computers. We're so excited about this. And you get to print out your ID, and pick it up at the front desk. And--
<v Amy>在这个过程中，他们一直在极力推广的一件事是，他们安装了新的电脑。这些电脑旨在引导孩子们制作一张非常酷的身份证。他们对此都非常兴奋。我想这些电脑一定是前一天才刚投入使用的，因为他们一直在说——一定要用我们的身份证电脑。我们对此太兴奋了。你可以打印出你的身份证，然后在前台领取。而且——

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:04.700
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>对。

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:18.510
<v Amy>And so when we walk out onto the actual museum part, my brother and I walk around, and he shows me some of his favorite exhibits. And this staff member comes up to me, and he says, have you made your ID yet?
<v Amy>所以当我们走进博物馆的实际参观区域时，我和我弟弟四处转了转，他给我展示了一些他最喜欢的展品。有个工作人员走过来对我说，你做身份证了吗？

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:38.430
<v Ira Glass>Now, for a second, it seems a little weird that he would ask one of the adults to make an ID card. But Amy figured it's new. They're excited for everybody to try this thing, kids and adults. So her brother ambles off, and she sits down and starts answering the questions on her computer screen. And the guy doesn't go away. He keeps finding ways to help her.
<v Ira Glass>现在，有一秒钟，我觉得他问一个成年人去制作身份证有点奇怪。但艾米觉得这是新东西，他们很兴奋，想让每个人，包括孩子和大人都试试。于是她弟弟慢悠悠地走开了，她坐下来开始回答电脑屏幕上的问题。那个男人并没有走开，他一直在找各种方法来帮助她。

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:41.630
<v Amy>Then it occurred to me, oh, well, maybe he's hitting on me a little bit.
<v Amy>然后我突然想到，哦，好吧，也许他是在跟我搭讪。

00:02:41.630 --> 00:03:11.830
<v Ira Glass>Which would make sense, but the help that he's giving her keeps getting stranger and stranger. He's giving her instructions for things that no fully functioning adult would ever need instructions for. And Amy wonders, does he think that she is one of the developmentally disabled kids who are wandering all over the museum? But at the same time, Amy thought it was completely obvious that she wasn't-- the way she moved, the way she expressed herself. And the guy's manner, his tone of voice was unmistakable. He talked to her like a peer.
<v Ira Glass>这说得通，但他给她的帮助却越来越奇怪。他给她的指示是任何心智健全的成年人根本不需要指点的东西。艾米不禁怀疑，他是不是以为她也是在博物馆里到处晃悠的发育障碍儿童之一？但同时，艾米认为她看起来完全不像——她的动作方式、她的表达方式。而且那个男人的举止、语气都非常明显。他把她当成同龄人来对待。

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:34.170
<v Amy>I mean, I almost wonder if maybe that was why I was almost paralyzed with confusion. It's really rare to meet people who don't use some kind of special voice with the mentally disabled, this slightly higher pitched, slightly "I know you're a special person" kind of voice. So it was just-- it was so strange.
<v Amy>我是说，我甚至怀疑这可能就是我当时被困惑到几乎瘫痪的原因。很少遇到人们在面对智障人士时不使用那种特殊的语调，那种稍微提高一点音调、那种“我知道你是特殊人士”的语调。所以这真的很奇怪。

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:45.490
<v Ira Glass>And then luckily, the very next question that comes up on the computer screen is one that Amy realizes can resolve all of this confusion once and for all, definitively, for the both of them.
<v Ira Glass>幸运的是，电脑屏幕上出现的下一个问题让艾米意识到，这个问题可以一劳永逸地为他们双方彻底解决这种困惑。

00:03:45.490 --> 00:04:22.110
<v Amy>So the question is, type in your age. OK, so I'm 21 years old at the time. So I'm thinking, I can type in 21, and he'll realize that I can't be a student. And we can just both pretend like we understood what was going on this whole time. So I type in 21. And he looks at me, and he looks at the age. And he says, oh, Amy, you know, if your age is a number that ends in "teen," then that number starts with a 1.
<v Amy>问题是，输入你的年龄。好吧，当时我21岁。所以我心想，我可以输入21，他就会意识到我不可能还是个学生。我们可以假装我们一直都明白是怎么回事。于是我输入了21。他看着我，又看了看年龄。然后他说，哦，艾米，你知道，如果你的年龄是一个以“teen”结尾的数字，那么这个数字是以1开头的。

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:24.430
<v Ira Glass>So he just assumes you've typed wrong.
<v Ira Glass>所以他直接假定你输错了。

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:44.710
<v Amy>Yeah. But I haven't. So at that point, I decide that instead of saying, OK, oh, actually, I am 21, and then letting that fold out however it would have, I erased what I typed, and I type in 18.
<v Amy>是的。但我没输错。所以在那一刻，我决定与其说，哦，其实我21岁了，然后让事情往不知名的方向发展，不如擦掉我输的，改成了18。

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:46.590
<v Ira Glass>Why?
<v Ira Glass>为什么？

00:04:46.590 --> 00:05:04.360
<v Amy>Ah-- I don't know. I mean, I thought, how many more questions could there possibly be? I've already told them everything about myself, how much I weigh. And then I thought, well, this is just going to be a couple more questions. And then it'll take a photo of me, and then I'll be done. And then he'll never have to know.
<v Amy>啊……我不知道。我是想，还能有多少个问题呢？我已经把我的一切都告诉他了，连体重都说了。然后我想，好吧，这大概只需要再回答几个问题。接着拍张照，我就完事了。那样他就永远不必知道了。

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:09.360
<v Ira Glass>He's a stranger. You're in a museum. This will be over in a minute. You can walk away. Who cares?
<v Ira Glass>他是个陌生人。你在博物馆。这一切一分钟就结束了。你可以走开。谁在乎呢？

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:15.680
<v Amy>Exactly. I felt like it was the lesser of two evils at that point.
<v Amy>确实。我觉得在那一刻，这是两害相权取其轻。

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:25.500
<v Ira Glass>But she is not near the end of the questionnaire-- far from it. It continues for pages and pages. Then there's a set of paragraph-length questions, which require actual concentration.
<v Ira Glass>但她离问卷结束还远着呢——差得远。接下来还有好几页。然后是一组需要真正集中注意力的长段落问题。

00:05:25.500 --> 00:06:02.920
<v Amy>And while I'm-- fast as I can, I'm skimming these questions, trying to get through these, he starts reading them to me out loud, word for word. And I'm sort of melting into my chair, and I'm thinking, this is too much. This is way too much of a deception. I have to somehow stop him. So I did. I said, oh, I can read. And then he looks at me. He puts his hand on my back, and he says, your parents must be so proud of you.
<v Amy>当我正尽可能快地扫视这些问题，试图做完它们时，他开始大声地逐字念给我听。我简直快要在椅子上融化了，心想，这太过分了。这种欺骗也太过了。我必须设法阻止他。所以我这么做了。我说，哦，我会认字。然后他看着我。他把手放在我的背上说，你的父母一定为你感到非常骄傲。

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:05.420
<v Ira Glass>[CHUCKLES]
<v Ira Glass>[轻笑]

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:27.700
<v Amy>So as you might imagine, I'm working really hard to skim through these questions as quick-- I'm a blur of activity. I'm just trying to get through these questions so I can get out of this room and escape. And as I'm working through these questions as fast as I can, I see my brother's teacher walking towards me. And she says, hey, Amy, how's it going?
<v Amy>你可以想象，我当时拼命想尽快扫完这些问题——我动作飞快。我只是想快点做完这些题，好离开这个房间逃走。当我正尽可能快地处理这些问题时，我看到我弟弟的老师朝我走来。她说，嘿，艾米，进展怎么样？

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:30.940
<v Ira Glass>And you're thinking like, oh my God, what's going to happen?
<v Ira Glass>你当时一定在想，天哪，会发生什么？

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:44.920
<v Amy>Oh. Oh. Yeah. And then it happens. He points to me, and he says-- again, voice full of admiration and pride. And he says, Amy was just telling me that she can read.
<v Amy>哦。哦。是的。然后事情发生了。他指着我说——语气再次充满了钦佩和自豪。他说，艾米刚才告诉我，她会认字。

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:50.380
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>[笑声]

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:52.760
<v Ira Glass>And how does the teacher take this news?
<v Ira Glass>老师听到这个消息后反应如何？

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:59.940
<v Amy>Oh my gosh! So this teacher, who I'd been complaining to about these graduate-level classes in German and--
<v Amy>天哪！所以这个老师，我之前一直跟她抱怨这些研究生水平的德语课，而且——

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:00.940
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>[笑声]

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:13.280
<v Amy>[SIGHS] She looks at him, and she says, of course Amy can read.
<v Amy>[叹气] 她看着他，说道，艾米当然会认字。

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:42.040
<v Ira Glass>And then the truth pours out. There begins a flurrying of apologizing on all sides, Amy apologizing to the guy, the guy apologizing to Amy, both of them, she says, clutching their own hearts as they do this. Turns out, he's so careful not to talk down to developmentally disabled students because he himself had a learning disability. And so she had misread what he was doing and why he spoke the way he did, and he misread who she really was. And in this case, they both discovered the truth.
<v Ira Glass>随后真相大白。双方开始了一阵慌乱的道歉，艾米向那个人道歉，那个人向艾米道歉，她说他们两人在道歉时都紧紧按着自己的胸口。原来，他之所以那么小心不去用那种居高临下的态度对待发育障碍学生，是因为他自己就有学习障碍。所以她误解了他的所作所为和说话方式的原因，而他也误解了她的真实身份。在这个案例中，他们最终都发现了真相。

00:07:42.040 --> 00:08:21.390
<v Ira Glass>But just as often, people meet and walk away and never straighten this stuff out. They assume. They get it all wrong. And that is the subject of today's program. From Chicago Public Radio, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's show, I've Got You Pegged-- stories of people assuming all kinds of things about other people, usually in error. Our show today in four acts, and it is quite a lineup-- Richard Price, David Rakoff, Shalom Auslander, and Nancy Updike. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>但同样常见的是，人们见面后各奔东西，从未澄清这些误会。他们假定。他们全搞错了。而这就是今天节目的主题。来自芝加哥公共广播电台，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目主题是“看透你了”——讲述人们对他人做出各种假设，通常都是错误的。我们今天的节目分为四幕，阵容相当强大——理查德·普莱斯、大卫·拉科夫、沙洛姆·奥斯兰德和南希·厄普代克。请继续收听。

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:56.110
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. Today's show is a rerun. Act 1, The Fat Blue Line. We begin today with a story about something that happens all the time in all kinds of places, something that is so common that it's not even big enough to make it on the local news. Richard Price tells the story, the novelist and screenwriter. He often writes about cops and crime. He wrote Clockers. He wrote Sea of Love. He wrote for the TV show The Wire. He told this story on stage at The Moth in New York back in 2008. He describes it as a tale taken from real life and dramatized.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。今天的节目是重播。第一幕，肥胖的蓝色防线。我们今天从一个在各种地方时常发生的故事开始，这件事太常见了，以至于连地方新闻都上不了。理查德·普莱斯讲述了这个故事，他是位小说家和编剧。他经常写警察和犯罪题材。他写过《Clockers》。他写过《海之恋》。他为电视剧《火线》写过剧本。他于2008年在纽约的“Moth”舞台上讲述了这个故事。他将其描述为一个取材于真实生活并戏剧化的故事。

00:08:56.110 --> 00:09:17.610
<v Richard Price>In the last novel I wrote, I spent a lot of time on the Lower East Side. And as is my wont, I wound up in the back of police cars a lot. And the Lower East Side is a very low-crime area right now. It used to be the worst. But Giuliani and real estate pressure took care of that. And now they basically have nothing to do down there in terms of crime.
<v Richard Price>在我写的上一部小说里，我在下东区待了很长时间。按照我的习惯，我最后经常坐在警车后座。下东区现在是一个犯罪率很低的地区。过去那是这里最糟糕的地方。但朱利安尼和房地产压力解决了这个问题。现在他们在犯罪方面基本没什么事可做了。

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:45.450
<v Richard Price>So what they do is they sort of sit in fake taxis. Four beefy white guys sit in a fake taxi by the side of Williamsburg Bridge, and they eyeball what's coming over from Brooklyn. And if the car looks like a $200 shitbox or somebody's got an Afro or a ponytail, they pull up, pull in behind the car, and they wait to see if the guy's going to go all polite in his driving, like put on lane change signals. Then they know he's dirty.
<v Richard Price>所以他们做的事就是坐在假出租车里。四个壮实的白人坐在威廉斯堡大桥旁的一辆假出租车里，盯着从布鲁克林过来的车。如果那辆车看起来像一辆价值200美元的烂车，或者车主留着爆炸头或马尾辫，他们就会开车跟上去，跟在车后面，等着看那个人开车是否表现得特别有礼貌，比如打转向灯。那时候他们就知道那个人有问题。

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:47.870
<v Richard Price>[LAUGHTER]
<v Richard Price>[笑声]

00:09:47.870 --> 00:10:03.790
<v Richard Price>And so worst comes to worse, it's fishing, really. It's a big fishing hole, Delancey Street. And so I spent all night in this bogus taxi with about 850 pounds of white beef.
<v Richard Price>所以最坏的情况，其实就是钓鱼。德兰西街是一个巨大的鱼塘。所以我在那辆假的士里和大约850磅的白人肌肉男待了一整晚。

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:06.330
<v Richard Price>[LAUGHTER]
<v Richard Price>[笑声]

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:33.930
<v Richard Price>And it's the end of the night. They made their collars. And there are two cops up front, and I'm sitting in the back. When I ride with these guys, I don't really comment about what they do. I don't engage them in any kind of debates. It's like, I'm there to bear witness and then see what I can do with it in my work. Anyways, they're riding up Essex Street. It's kind of Miller time.
<v Richard Price>那天快结束了。他们完成了抓捕。前面坐着两个警察，我坐在后面。当我和这些人在一起时，我通常不对他们的所作所为发表评论。我不参与任何辩论。就像是，我在那儿见证，然后看看我能在作品中如何运用它。不管怎样，他们开车沿着埃塞克斯街行驶。差不多到了下班时间。

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:36.360
<v Richard Price>[LAUGHTER]
<v Richard Price>[笑声]

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:58.040
<v Richard Price>And as they're going up, they pass a Black guy about 30 years old with dreads on a bicycle. And on the crossbars, he's got a white kid about 9, 10 years old. And the Black guy and the white kid, they're kind of chatting. The kid's looking up at the guy. They look like they're sort of familiar with each other.
<v Richard Price>当他们往上走时，他们经过一个30岁左右、留着脏辫的黑人，他正骑着自行车。在横梁上坐着一个9、10岁左右的白人小孩。那个黑人和白人小孩正在聊天。孩子抬头看着那个人。他们看起来好像很熟悉对方。

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:18.580
<v Richard Price>And the cops drive by, and they're dead silent. After about a block, one guy says to the other, says, hey, big guy, does that look fishy to you? He says, it's fucking midnight. What's going on here? They said, well, what do you want to do, big guy? So I'll tell you, big guy. All right, make the light. Pull over. Let's see what's what.
<v Richard Price>警察开车经过时，沉默得可怕。过了一个街区，一个人对另一个人说，嘿，大块头，那看起来可疑吗？他说，这都半夜了。这到底是怎么回事？他们说，那你想怎么办，大块头？我告诉你吧，大块头。好了，抓个现行。靠边停车。看看是怎么回事。

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:34.740
<v Richard Price>So they pull over. Bike's coming up Essex. One of the cops steps in the road, puts his hand down, and says, hey, how you doing? Get off the bike, please. And the Black guy gets off the bike, and he goes, hey, officers, like it's an unexpected treat-- you know, hi, what's up?
<v Richard Price>于是他们靠边停车。自行车沿着埃塞克斯街上来。其中一个警察走到路中间，把手压下，说，嘿，你好吗？请下车。那个黑人从自行车上下来，说，嘿，警官，就像是一个意外的惊喜——你知道，嗨，怎么了？

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:37.620
<v Richard Price>[LAUGHTER]
<v Richard Price>[笑声]

00:11:37.620 --> 00:12:00.640
<v Richard Price>And he says, did you ever hear of helmets? Oh, yeah. Gee, I'm really sorry. At which point, the other cop says to the little white kid, he says, hey, big guy, what's your name? He goes, um, Noah Rosenberg? Like he's not sure. He says, hey, Noah, come here, buddy. Come on over here. And he separates the two. And I'm sort of hopping in between the two conversations at this point.
<v Richard Price>他说，你听说过头盔吗？哦，是啊。天哪，真的很抱歉。就在这时，另一个警察对那个白人小男孩说，嘿，大块头，你叫什么名字？他说，嗯，诺亚·罗森伯格？好像他不确定。他说，嘿，诺亚，过来，哥们。过来这边。他把两人分开了。而我此时在两段对话之间来回穿梭。

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:20.720
<v Richard Price>And the Black guy tries to follow the white kid, and the other cop puts his hand on his chest and he says, no, you stay over here. Let me see some ID. He says, what? He says, some ID. He says, don't look at him. Look at me. He says, no, no, I was just picking him up from a play date. Did I ask you that? No, no. You don't understand. I work the bar at Schiller's.
<v Richard Price>黑人试图跟着白人小孩，另一个警察把手放在他的胸口说，不，你留在这儿。给我看点证件。他说，什么？他说，证件。他说，别看他。看我。他说，不，不，我只是刚接他参加完游戏聚会。我问你那个了吗？没有，没有。你不明白。我在席勒酒吧工作。

00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:47.660
<v Richard Price>And again, did I ask you that? Well, no. He said, why are you trying to divert me? He said, go down the same road as me. He goes, OK, OK. And he gives him the ID, and at which point, he sort of waves to the kid. And he goes, oh, what did I just say to you? He says, no, no, I'm really sorry. It's Noah. He's kind of wound a little tight. He says, oh, really? He says, have a seat, and he points to the curb. He makes the guy sit on the curb with his feet in the gutter.
<v Richard Price>又来了，我问你那个了吗？好吧，没有。他说，你为什么要转移我的注意力？他说，跟我走同一条路。他说，好，好。他给了他证件，就在这时，他向那个孩子招了招手。他说，哦，我刚才跟你说什么来着？他说，不，不，我真的很抱歉。这是诺亚。他有点紧张。他说，哦，真的吗？他说，坐下，并指着路缘石。他让那个人坐在路缘石上，脚放在排水沟里。

00:12:47.660 --> 00:13:04.680
<v Richard Price>And he says, so where are you going? He says, well, I had the late shift. And he says, it's kind of late to be driving around with a kid on a bike, isn't it? At which point, I leave those two, and I go over to Noah and the other cop. And he says, so your name's Noah, huh? And the kid says, yes. And for the millionth time, I don't have an ark.
<v Richard Price>他说，你们去哪儿？他说，哦，我上了晚班。他说，大半夜骑自行车带着个孩子，不太合适吧？就在这时，我离开了那两个，走向诺亚和另一个警察。他说，所以你叫诺亚，是吧？孩子说，是的。而且第一百万次了，我没有方舟。

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:07.380
<v Richard Price>[LAUGHTER]
<v Richard Price>[笑声]

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:33.800
<v Richard Price>And the cop says, boy, you must get that a lot. The kid goes, oh, my God, you have no idea. And he says, so Noah, how old are you? He says, well, next week, I'll be a decade old. Well, that's great. Great. And where do you live? He says, 333 Avenue B. He says, and you go to school around here? He says, yes, I go to the Earth School. And he says, oh, cool. And who do you live with? He says, I live with my mom. And he goes, who's that over there?
<v Richard Price>警察说，孩子，你肯定常听这个吧。孩子说，天哪，你根本不知道。他说，那么诺亚，你几岁了？他说，嗯，下周我就十岁了。哦，太棒了。太好了。你住哪儿？他说，B大道333号。他说，你在这附近上学吗？他说，是的，我上地球学校。他说，哦，酷。你和谁住在一起？他说，我和妈妈住。他说，那边那个人是谁？

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:45.160
<v Richard Price>He says, well, I don't know who your friend is, but my friend is Cleve. And he says, oh, do you know what Cleve's last name is? He says, yeah, Carter. Cleve Carter. Sometimes I call him Coca-Cola. Sometimes I call him Carbon Copy.
<v Richard Price>他说，好吧，我不知道你的朋友是谁，但我的朋友是克里夫。他说，哦，你知道克里夫的姓吗？他说，知道，卡特。克里夫·卡特。有时我叫他可口可乐。有时我叫他复印件。

00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:46.350
<v Richard Price>[LAUGHTER]
<v Richard Price>[笑声]

00:13:46.350 --> 00:14:10.130
<v Richard Price>He says, oh, do you know him for long? I said, well, yeah, like 1, 1 and 1/4 years. He's kind of like my godfather since my other godfather died. Oh. And what do you guys do? Well, he was taking me home from a play date to my mom's. Says, so he knows your mom? And he says, well, yeah, he and my mom are kind of like friends. He says, well, kind of like? Well, they have sleepover dates.
<v Richard Price>他说，你们认识很久了吗？我说，嗯，是的，大概一年零三个月吧。他就像我的教父，因为我另一个教父去世了。哦。你们在一起做什么？嗯，他正带我从游戏聚会回我妈妈那里。说，所以他认识你妈妈？他说，嗯，是啊，他和我的妈妈就像是朋友。他说，嗯，像什么朋友？嗯，他们有留宿约会。

00:14:10.130 --> 00:14:11.470
<v Richard Price>[LAUGHTER]
<v Richard Price>[笑声]

00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:37.470
<v Richard Price>And he says, so your mom knows that you're with him now? He says, well, my mom sent him to pick me up. My dad lives in Woodstock. He said, OK, at which point, I'm going, OK, let's see what's happening with Cleve. I walk over there. Cleve's sitting on-- he's sitting on the curb, and he's got his feet out there. And he's trying to make it look like it's natural. So he's massaging his deep thigh muscles like he's limbering up for the marathon or something. It's just humiliating as shit.
<v Richard Price>他说，所以你妈妈知道你现在跟他在一起吗？他说，嗯，我妈妈派他来接我的。我爸爸住在伍德斯托克。他说，好吧，就在这时，我在想，好吧，看看克里夫那边发生了什么。我走过去。克里夫坐在路缘石上，双脚伸在那里。他正试图让这一切看起来很自然。所以他正在按摩他的大腿肌肉，就像是在为马拉松热身一样。这真是丢人到家了。

00:14:37.470 --> 00:14:55.050
<v Richard Price>And he's kind of smiling because you can't win. You got to play through. And he's sitting there. And the other cop is hitting his driver's license with a mag light, one of those big powerful flashlights. And he goes, so Cleveland, I see you're from Ohio. And he says--
<v Richard Price>他还在微笑，因为你赢不了。你只能硬着头皮演下去。他坐在那儿。另一个警察正用强光手电筒照着他的驾照，就是那种功率很大的手电筒。他说，所以克利夫兰，我看到你是俄亥俄州的。他说——

00:14:55.050 --> 00:14:56.890
<v Richard Price>[LAUGHTER]
<v Richard Price>[笑声]

00:14:56.890 --> 00:15:26.570
<v Richard Price>He says, yeah. He says, Cleveland from Ohio. He says, well, actually, it's from Oxford. And the guy goes, oh, Miami College. And he goes, yeah, yeah, that's where I went. And the cop goes, oh, Wally Szczerbiak, who's this big basketball player. And he goes, well, yeah, Wally was a little before my time. He said, oh, did you play ball for them, too? He said, well, not basketball, I played soccer. Oh, that's amazing because I teach soccer. I coach soccer out on the island, the kids' league. And he says, that's amazing. I keep waiting for that sport to blow up. But I don't think it's-- you know.
<v Richard Price>他说，是的。他说，来自俄亥俄州的克利夫兰。他说，好吧，其实是来自牛津。那个人说，哦，迈阿密大学。他说，对，对，那是我去的地方。警察说，哦，沃利·斯泽比亚克，那个很有名的大篮球运动员。他说，嗯，对，沃利比我那一届稍微早一点。他说，哦，你也为他们打球吗？他说，嗯，不是篮球，我踢足球。哦，那太棒了，因为我教足球。我在岛上教足球，带儿童联赛。他说，那太棒了。我一直在等那个运动火起来。但我觉得它……你知道。

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:51.610
<v Richard Price>And Cleveland's going, yeah, yeah, that's amazing, sitting just like this, at which point a Mustang comes by with two Black guys in it and-- up Essex. And the guy in the shotgun seat looks out the window, and he sees Cleveland sitting on the curb. And he starts yelling out, homeboy to base, homeboy to base. We got a Black man down. I repeat, a Black man down.
<v Richard Price>克利夫兰说，是啊，是啊，那太棒了，就像这样坐着，就在这时，一辆野马跑车载着两个黑人沿着埃塞克斯街过来了。坐在副驾驶位置的人向窗外看，看到克利夫兰坐在路缘石上。他开始大喊，伙计呼叫基地，伙计呼叫基地。我们抓到一个黑人了。重复一遍，一个黑人。

00:15:51.610 --> 00:15:52.230
<v Richard Price>[LAUGHTER]
<v Richard Price>[笑声]

00:15:52.230 --> 00:16:17.000
<v Richard Price>And he's laughing his ass off. And the Mustang floors it. And Cleveland's kind of squinting, and he's kind of looking the other way. He's just like, mortified. And hopping back to the other cop with the kid, he says, so Noah, does Cleveland live with you? He goes, no, Cleveland lives at 444 Avenue D. We live at 332 Avenue B.
<v Richard Price>他笑得不行。野马车全速开走了。克利夫兰眯着眼，转过头去。他简直感到羞愧难当。回到带着那个孩子的警察那里，他说，所以诺亚，克利夫兰和你住一起吗？他回答，不，克利夫兰住在D大道444号。我们住在B大道332号。

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:46.720
<v Richard Price>He says, well, you ever been over to his house? He says, only about a million times. And he says, mostly with your mom, I guess, huh? He says, and by myself. He said, oh, really? What do you do there? And he says, well, sometimes we walk his dog. It's a Rhodesian Ridgeback named Mars. And one time, he tried to teach me how to make scrambled eggs. But I don't really like his oven because you light the pilot match, and it goes, fwoosh. And it scares me.
<v Richard Price>他说，那么你去过他家吗？他说，大概有一百万次了吧。他说，大部分时间是和你妈妈一起吧，嗯？他说，还有我自己去。他说，哦，真的吗？你在那儿做什么？他说，嗯，有时我们去遛他的狗。是一只叫马斯的罗德西亚背脊犬。有一次，他教我怎么做炒蛋。但我不怎么喜欢他的烤箱，因为你要点燃那个引火火柴，然后它会“呼”地一声。那吓到我了。

00:16:46.720 --> 00:17:18.400
<v Richard Price>And he says, one time, my mom had to go to court in Woodstock, and I stayed with Cleveland for three days. And he said, in court, huh? He said, yeah. Three days? He said, yeah. He said, but mostly, I'd say 82 and 1/2% of the time we watch television. You and Cleveland? He said, yeah, me and Cleveland. He goes-- and the cop said to him, do you ever do anything else with him? And he goes, what? What do you mean? He goes, do you do anything else with him?
<v Richard Price>他说，有一次，我妈妈不得不去伍德斯托克出庭，我在克利夫兰那里住了三天。他说，出庭，嗯？他说，是的。三天？他说，是的。他说，但大部分时间，我会说82.5%的时间我们都在看电视。你和克利夫兰？他说，是的，我和克利夫兰。他说……警察对他说，你除了这些还和他做别的事吗？他问，什么？你什么意思？他问，你还和他做别的事吗？

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:54.380
<v Richard Price>And all of a sudden, the kid's eyes get really big and kind of wet, like steel. And the kid starts kind of-- kind of breathing kind of heavy. And the cop starts shaking a little bit. And the cop says to him, he says, hey, Noah, look at this. And he pulls his jacket back, and he shows him his detective shield on his belt. He says, do you know what that is? He says, yeah, it's a police badge. He says, you know what this means? He says, what? He says, that means you can tell me anything you want, and you'll be perfectly safe. Do you understand that?
<v Richard Price>突然，孩子的眼睛变得很大，有点湿润，像钢一样。孩子开始有点呼吸沉重。警察开始有点发抖。警察对他说，嘿，诺亚，看这个。他拉开他的夹克，露出腰带上的侦探徽章。他说，你知道那是什么吗？他说，知道，那是警徽。他说，你知道这意味着什么吗？他说，什么？他说，这意味着你可以告诉我任何你想说的事，你会非常安全。你明白吗？

00:17:54.380 --> 00:18:13.860
<v Richard Price>And the kid looks at him, and he goes, oh, my god, are you going to arrest him? And the cop-- his heart's pumping Kool-Aid, and he starts moving over. And he says, why? And the kid goes, if you fucking assholes arrest him again one more time just because he's Black, and I'm not, I'm going to kill myself.
<v Richard Price>孩子看着他说，天哪，你要逮捕他吗？警察——他的心跳加速，开始靠近。他说，为什么？孩子说，如果你们这些混蛋因为他是黑人、我不是，而再次逮捕他，我就自杀。

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:29.880
<v Richard Price>You came into my apartment and dragged him out because the crazy lady next door said he was a rapist. You put him in handcuffs when he came to pick me up at school. You pulled him away from me at the street fair and made me wait for my mom. I swear to God, I'm going to lose my mind.
<v Richard Price>你们闯进我家公寓把他强行带走，仅仅因为隔壁那个疯女人说他是强奸犯。当他来学校接我时，你们给他戴上了手铐。你们在街头集市把他从我身边拉走，让我等我妈妈。我发誓，我快要疯了。

00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:56.380
<v Richard Price>And the cops go, whoa, easy, easy, easy. At which point, both cops are looking at each other like, well, what's going on? And all of a sudden, Cleveland sees the kid's losing it, and he goes, hey, Noah, buddy. And the cop goes, what did I just say to you? Stop talking to the kid. And at which point, Cleveland says, Officer, do you want to put this to rest? I tell you what. I'm reaching for my cell phone in here. He says, why don't you call-- why don't you call the kid's mom and just see what's going on?
<v Richard Price>警察说，哇，冷静，冷静，冷静。就在这时，两个警察互相看着，心想，这到底是怎么回事？突然，克利夫兰看到孩子要崩溃了，说，嘿，诺亚，哥们。警察说，我刚才跟你说什么来着？别跟孩子说话。就在这时，克利夫兰说，警官，你想结束这一切吗？我告诉你。我正伸手拿我的手机。他说，你为什么不打个电话——你为什么不给孩子的妈妈打个电话，看看是怎么回事？

00:18:56.380 --> 00:19:23.760
<v Richard Price>He says, I'll call the kid's mom. He says, what's her name? She goes, Idina. And he calls across to the other cop, get the kid's mother's name. And the kid, through sobs, is going, Idina. And Cleveland gives the cop the mother's number. The cop calls, and he says, hey, how you doing? This is Sergeant Daley from the 7th Precinct. Who am I speaking to? And she goes, oh, my god, Idina Rosenberg. What happened?
<v Richard Price>他说，我来打。他说，她叫什么名字？她叫伊迪娜。他对着另一个警察喊，问出孩子母亲的名字。孩子抽泣着说，伊迪娜。克利夫兰把母亲的号码给了警察。警察打了过去，说，嘿，你好吗？我是第七分局的戴利警长。请问是谁？她说，天哪，我是伊迪娜·罗森伯格。发生什么事了？

00:19:23.760 --> 00:19:42.970
<v Richard Price>He goes, nothing happened. I just need to know, do you know where your son is right now? At which point, she freaks. She goes, where is he? What happened? He's supposed to be with Cleveland. He's supposed to be taking him home from a play date. What happened? What happened? What happened? Well, no, no, no. He's fine. OK. At which point, the kid goes, my mom says, if I get any more nervous, I'm going to have to live with my father in Woodstock, you fuck!
<v Richard Price>他说，没发生什么。我只是需要知道，你知道你儿子现在在哪里吗？就在这时，她抓狂了。她说，他在哪儿？发生什么事了？他应该和克利夫兰在一起。他应该带他从游戏聚会回家的。怎么了？发生什么事了？发生什么事了？嗯，不，不，不。他很好。好的。就在这时，孩子说，我妈妈说，如果我再紧张一点，我就得搬去伍德斯托克和我爸爸住了，你这个混蛋！

00:19:42.970 --> 00:19:46.090
<v Richard Price>[LAUGHTER]
<v Richard Price>[笑声]

00:19:46.090 --> 00:20:13.530
<v Richard Price>And the cops are going, no, no, no, the kid's great. The kid's fine. The only thing is, they were riding without helmets. And it's a serious safety violation. And she's just going, oh, my god. Oh, Jesus. She says, no, no, no. OK, don't worry about it. All right. All right. All right. Good night. Have a good night, and hangs up. At which point, they bring Cleveland and Noah together again, and they give them this half-assed lecture on bicycle safety.
<v Richard Price>警察们说，不，不，不，孩子很好。孩子没事。唯一的问题是，他们没戴头盔骑车。这是严重的交通安全违规。而她只是说，天哪。哦，上帝。她说，不，不，不。好的，别担心。好了。好了。好了。晚安。祝晚安，然后挂断了电话。就在这时，他们把克利夫兰和诺亚聚在一起，给他们上了一堂敷衍了事的自行车安全课。

00:20:13.530 --> 00:20:37.830
<v Richard Price>And he says, I'm supposed to write you up, but I'm going to give you a pass this time. And Cleveland's still kind of smiling, but the smile doesn't go past here. It never reaches his eyes. And he gets back on the bike, and the kid gets on the handlebars. And the kid's going through that post-crying jag, shudder withdrawal. And Cleveland's kind of talking him down as he sort of pushes off, and they go disappear up Essex.
<v Richard Price>他说，我本应该给你们开罚单，但这次放你们一马。克利夫兰仍然微笑着，但微笑没到眼底。它从未传达到他的眼睛里。他骑上自行车，孩子坐上车把。孩子还在经历那种哭泣后的阵颤。克利夫兰一边推车离开，一边安抚他，他们消失在埃塞克斯街上。

00:20:37.830 --> 00:20:55.850
<v Richard Price>And we get back in the police car. And I'm sitting in the back. I'm not saying a fucking thing. And they go in dead silence for about two blocks. And one cop finally says to the other, he says, you know something, big guy? And the other guy says, what's that, big guy? He says, still feels fishy to me.
