<v Richard Price>[掌声，欢呼声]

00:21:07.310 --> 00:21:21.750
<v Ira Glass>Richard Price. His latest novel is called Lazarus Man. The story was recorded by The Moth, which, of course, is a podcast with all kinds of stories like the one you just heard at themoth.org, or find it wherever you get your podcasts.
<v Ira Glass>理查德·普莱斯。他的最新小说叫《拉撒路人》。这个故事由“Moth”录制，当然，这是一个拥有各种像你刚才听到的那种故事的播客，网址是themoth.org，或者在任何你获取播客的地方都能找到。

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:45.810
<v Ira Glass>Act 2, Yes, No, or Baby. Well, there's a simple rule designed to help us all fight the urge to pigeonhole other people based on a first impression. And I speak, of course, of don't judge a book by its cover. But this axiom, this adage, this maxim, is deeply flawed, and not just because of its annoyingly scoldy self-righteousness.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，是，不是，还是宝贝。好吧，有一个简单的规则，旨在帮助我们所有人抵制根据第一印象去把他人归类的冲动。我指的当然是不要以貌取人。但这个公理、这句格言、这句箴言，是有严重缺陷的，不仅仅是因为它那种令人讨厌的唠叨般的自以为是。

00:21:45.810 --> 00:22:00.330
<v Ira Glass>The fact is, there are times in life where you must judge a person based on woefully little information crammed into some cover letter, or something like that, that they have put together. One of our show's producers, Nancy Updike, visited somebody in this very judgy situation.
<v Ira Glass>事实是，人生中有时你必须根据求职信中挤出的极其有限的信息，或其他类似的东西来判断一个人。我们节目的制片人之一，南希·厄普代克，就在这种充满判断力的情况下探访了一个人。

00:22:00.330 --> 00:22:18.970
<v Nancy Updike>Anyone who's not a megalomaniac feels daunted by the prospect of having to separate a group of strangers into a yes pile and a no pile. Kim Schwary is seven months pregnant, and she's facing on her coffee table in Seattle dozens of one-page letters from couples who want to adopt her baby.
<v Nancy Updike>任何不是自大狂的人，在想到要把一群陌生人分成“是”和“否”两堆时，都会感到畏缩。金·施瓦里怀孕七个月了，她正面对着西雅图自家咖啡桌上数十封想领养她孩子的夫妇写的求职信。

00:22:18.970 --> 00:22:36.200
<v Nancy Updike>Individually, each letter seems like not enough information, and collectively, they are way, way too much. The first time she read through them, they melted into an indistinguishable mass of thoughtful, intelligent, fun-loving, mutually supportive, healthy, active, outdoorsy couples.
<v Nancy Updike>单独看，每一封信似乎信息都不够，而合在一起看，信息又实在太多了。她第一次读的时候，它们融合成了难以区分的一堆，全都是体贴、聪明、热爱生活、互相支持、健康、积极、热爱户外的夫妇。

00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:48.940
<v Kim Schwary>A lot of the hobbies are similar, just hiking, couples from the Northwest, too, I'm sure that's common. So like even just this page-- hiking, snowshoeing, camping.
<v Kim Schwary>很多爱好都一样，就是徒步旅行，西北地区的夫妇也一样，我相信这很常见。所以连这一页也是——徒步旅行、雪鞋健行、露营。

00:22:48.940 --> 00:22:52.500
<v Nancy Updike>And if you turn to the next page, is there also a hiking part?
<v Nancy Updike>如果你翻到下一页，有没有徒步旅行的部分？

00:22:52.500 --> 00:22:59.620
<v Kim Schwary>Let's see. Probably. Oh, yes, hiking. There you go. [LAUGHS]
<v Kim Schwary>看看。可能有。哦，有，徒步旅行。你看。[笑声]

00:22:59.620 --> 00:23:23.140
<v Nancy Updike>Kim's doing an open adoption, which means that she will have a relationship with the family who adopts her child for the rest of her life. The cover letters are just the beginning of a long, long process. The whole thing is sort of like super high stakes online dating. Judging people is required. Kim goes back and forth between practical considerations and pure gut checks.
<v Nancy Updike>金正在进行开放式领养，这意味着她余生都将与领养她孩子的家庭保持联系。求职信只是漫长过程的开始。这一切就像是超高风险的在线约会。必须去评判别人。金在实际考虑和纯粹的直觉判断之间摇摆不定。

00:23:23.140 --> 00:23:43.600
<v Kim Schwary>Here's something. This couple has another child, and they're talking-- I don't know. They use kind of like child talk when they're talking about him. Like, it says something about "kissing his boo-boos" or something. And that kind of-- I don't know. I don't like that very much.
<v Kim Schwary>看看这个。这对夫妇有另一个孩子，他们在谈论……我不知道。他们在谈论他时用了某种孩子气的语言。比如，上面写着关于“亲吻他的小伤口”之类的。那种……我不知道。我不太喜欢那个。

00:23:43.600 --> 00:24:01.140
<v Nancy Updike>Kim ends up liking a different couple that already has a little boy. She likes the idea that her baby would have a sibling. She weeds out a family that seems to her too religious. And later, she frowns on a couple who both have high-powered jobs that they write about at length. She wonders if they'd be home enough for the baby.
<v Nancy Updike>金最后喜欢上了另一对已经有个小男孩的夫妇。她喜欢她的孩子能有个兄弟姐妹的想法。她排除掉了一个她觉得太宗教的家庭。后来，她对一对都拥有高压职业的夫妇表示反感，信中花了大量篇幅写工作。她怀疑他们是否能有足够的时间陪孩子。

00:24:01.140 --> 00:24:06.240
<v Kim Schwary>These guys, I could see them working 50, 60 hours a week because of their careers.
<v Kim Schwary>这些人，我能看出他们因为职业原因会每周工作50、60个小时。

00:24:06.240 --> 00:24:11.080
<v Nancy Updike>A few pages later, Kim makes a face when a couple says they're "relational people."
<v Nancy Updike>几页之后，当一对夫妇说他们是“重关系的人”时，金露出了厌恶的表情。

00:24:11.080 --> 00:24:44.960
<v Kim Schwary>I'm wondering exactly what that meant. There's this whole packet of information, and then I'll focus on this sentence that maybe I don't like. And that makes me feel like I'm really kind of judgmental when I do this. And sometimes that doesn't always feel good. See, and this is something really small, but for some reason, it's-- it says, "the opportunity to have a relationship with the child's biological heritage is very important, including medical history." I just-- that feels kind of weird to me.
<v Kim Schwary>我在想那到底是什么意思。有一整包信息，然后我就会盯着这句话，可能我不喜欢它。这让我觉得自己这样做时真的很势利。有时这感觉并不好。看，这只是件小事，但出于某种原因……它上面写着，“拥有与孩子生物血缘关系建立联系的机会非常重要，包括医疗记录。”我只是……这对我来说感觉有点奇怪。

00:24:44.960 --> 00:24:45.650
<v Nancy Updike>It's a little clinical.
<v Nancy Updike>有点临床冷漠。

00:24:45.650 --> 00:24:53.020
<v Kim Schwary>Right, it is a little clinical. I want to be more than medical records, so.
<v Kim Schwary>对，是有点临床冷漠。我想成为不仅仅是医疗记录，所以。

00:24:53.020 --> 00:24:55.340
<v Nancy Updike>So that kind of put you off right there?
<v Nancy Updike>所以那种说法让你感到反感了吗？

00:24:55.340 --> 00:25:08.660
<v Kim Schwary>Yeah. So it goes back to that judgmental thing, you know? Because I mean, I've written things, and maybe that's not how they meant to portray themselves, but it does, like you said, sound very clinical, and-- yeah.
<v Kim Schwary>是的。所以这又回到了那种评判心理，你知道吗？因为我的意思是，我也写过东西，也许他们并不是那样想表达自己，但就像你说的，听起来非常临床冷漠，而且……是的。

00:25:08.660 --> 00:25:32.890
<v Nancy Updike>Just like Kim's having to quickly peg these families, extrapolating all sorts of important things from just words on a page and a few pictures, she's finding that as a single woman, pregnant, people are making all kinds of assumptions about her. Co-workers assume she's married and starting a family. Those are awkward conversations. And if she tells someone what's really going on, that she's putting the baby up for adoption, she better be prepared for a lecture.
<v Nancy Updike>就像金不得不快速给这些家庭贴标签，仅从书面文字和几张照片中推断出各种重要事项一样，她发现作为一个未婚怀孕的单身女性，人们也在对她做出各种假设。同事们假设她已婚并开始组建家庭。那些对话很尴尬。如果她告诉别人到底发生了什么，她要把孩子送去领养，她最好做好被说教的准备。

00:25:32.890 --> 00:25:56.390
<v Kim Schwary>I mean, you're really kind of vulnerable when you're pregnant. And you're kind of-- you're vulnerable to judgment, I guess. People are very prone to-- once you say what you're doing, they tell you what they would do and not do. And they want to be like, oh, I think you're going to be really upset. And I think this. And I'm like, no, I think maybe you're the one who's upset, not me. But--
<v Kim Schwary>我是说，当你怀孕时，你真的很脆弱。而且你有点……我想你很容易受到评判。人们非常倾向于——一旦你说出你在做什么，他们就会告诉你他们会怎么做或不怎么做。他们想说，哦，我觉得你会很难过。我觉得这个那个。我心想，不，我觉得也许难过的是你，不是我。但是……

00:25:56.390 --> 00:26:07.390
<v Nancy Updike>She says some people are utterly convinced that adoption is just a bad idea all around, which Kim takes personally since she was adopted. It's one of the reasons she didn't want to get an abortion.
<v Nancy Updike>她说有些人完全确信领养在各方面都是个坏主意，金对此感到很受伤，因为她自己就是被领养的。这也是她不想堕胎的原因之一。

00:26:07.390 --> 00:26:27.510
<v Kim Schwary>People feel that if you're adopted that you have this great sense of loss, or you just don't know who you are, and there's some identity-- there's an identity crisis, and you feel abandoned. And that's not how I felt with my experience.
<v Kim Schwary>人们觉得如果你是被领养的，你会有种巨大的失落感，或者你不知道自己是谁，还有某种身份……存在身份危机，你感到被遗弃。但在我的经历中，我并没有那种感觉。

00:26:27.510 --> 00:26:28.330
<v Nancy Updike>Being adopted?
<v Nancy Updike>被领养？

00:26:28.330 --> 00:26:53.650
<v Kim Schwary>Being adopted, exactly. I felt very loved by my parents. The baby's father told me that he thought that-- I don't know why he said this, but he said that-- or he kind of assumed and said this, that he was so much closer to his parents than I was to mine, which isn't true at all. [LAUGHS] And it's just-- that's how he felt things must be.
<v Kim Schwary>被领养，没错。我感到父母非常爱我。孩子的父亲告诉我，他认为——我不知道他为什么要这么说，但他说了——或者他假设并说了，他与父母的关系比我与父母的关系亲近得多，这完全不是事实。[笑声] 这只是……那是他觉得事情必然的样子。

00:26:53.650 --> 00:27:06.670
<v Nancy Updike>I waited till just about the end of my interview with Kim to confess that I'd had her pegged all wrong before I spoke to her. I thought she'd be a teenager, confused. But Kim is 27, has her own apartment, a good job.
<v Nancy Updike>我等到采访快结束时才坦白，在和金交谈之前，我完全把她看错了。我以为她是个困惑的青少年。但金27岁了，有自己的公寓，有份好工作。

00:27:06.670 --> 00:27:14.650
<v Kim Schwary>Right. I'm in a situation where it would be very feasible that I would raise the child on my own. So it's probably harder to understand.
<v Kim Schwary>没错。我现在的情况是，独自抚养孩子是非常可行的。所以可能比较难理解。

00:27:14.650 --> 00:27:24.850
<v Nancy Updike>OK, so people are making that judgment about you. What don't they know about you that would make them understand?
<v Nancy Updike>好的，所以人们在对你做出那种判断。有什么关于你的事情是他们不知道的，如果知道的话会让他们理解？

00:27:24.850 --> 00:28:05.420
<v Kim Schwary>I don't know. I kind of have this unusual kind of circumstances that I was engaged, and my fiancé passed away. And after that, I kind of think I was lonely and looking for someone. I feel like I made a mistake, you know? I still miss my fiancé and think about him all the time. And I just-- I don't feel like I'm emotionally ready to raise a child. I feel like I could. But I just feel like it's not the right time. And--
<v Kim Schwary>我不知道。我有一种不寻常的情况，我曾经订过婚，但未婚夫去世了。在那之后，我想我是因为孤独而在寻找某人。我觉得我犯了个错，你知道吗？我依然想念我的未婚夫，一直想起他。我只是……我觉得我在情感上还没准备好抚养孩子。我觉得我能做到。但我就是觉得现在不是合适的时机。而且……

00:28:05.420 --> 00:28:11.330
<v Nancy Updike>Especially if it's something that you guys talked about doing together, and here you are.
<v Nancy Updike>尤其是如果那是你们曾讨论过一起做的事，而现在却变成这样。

00:28:11.330 --> 00:28:37.240
<v Kim Schwary>Right, exactly. I mean, we were always going to have kids together. And here I am, single and pregnant with some guy, who's just a kind of insignificant fellow. And it certainly isn't-- things aren't going in the way that they were supposed to or as planned. And-- right.
<v Kim Schwary>对，完全正确。我的意思是，我们原本打算一起要孩子的。而现在我却成了单身，怀着某个家伙的孩子，他只是一个无名小卒。这绝对不是……事情没有按照原本预想的那样发展。而且……对。

00:28:37.240 --> 00:28:59.260
<v Nancy Updike>Kim doesn't go into her reasons with most people, so they make assumptions about her that she doesn't bother to correct. And she flips through dozens of letters from couples and makes assumptions about them that they'll never get a chance to respond to. It's the way of the world. Everyone's a judge, and everyone's the accused. And every day, things aren't going the way they were supposed to or as planned.
<v Nancy Updike>金不会和大多数人谈论她的原因，所以他们会对她做出她懒得纠正的假设。她翻阅着几十封来自夫妇的信件，并对他们做出他们永远没机会反驳的假设。这就是世道。每个人都是法官，每个人也都是被告。每一天，事情都没有按照预想的那样发展。

00:28:59.260 --> 00:29:05.000
<v Nancy Updike>[ENGINE REVVING, PAGES FLIPPING]
<v Nancy Updike>[引擎加速声，翻页声]

00:29:05.000 --> 00:29:10.640
<v Ira Glass>Nancy Updike is one of the producers of our program.
<v Ira Glass>南希·厄普代克是我们节目的制片人之一。

00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:25.320
<v Ira Glass>Act 3, Isn't It Romantic? Well, our show today is about people jumping to conclusions about what others are like. And what we turn to now is corny, stereotyped ideas people have, and in this case, put into Broadway shows.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，难道不浪漫吗？好吧，我们今天的节目是关于人们对他人是什么样的人匆忙得出结论。我们现在要转向人们心中俗气、刻板的观念，在这种情况下，是关于百老汇演出。

00:29:25.320 --> 00:29:50.420
<v Ira Glass>This story is about a kind of iconic, old musical, the show Rent. And when that show came out in the 1990s, one of our contributors, David Rakoff, was not a fan. That show-- maybe you've seen it, or you've seen the movie-- was about New Yorkers in their 20s. David Rakoff had been a New Yorker in his 20s during the same era depicted in that show, and he thought the play made some mistakes.
<v Ira Glass>这个故事是关于一部标志性的老音乐剧《吉屋出租》（Rent）。当那部剧在90年代上映时，我们的一位撰稿人，大卫·拉科夫，并不是它的粉丝。那部剧——也许你看过，或者看过电影——是关于20多岁的纽约人。大卫·拉科夫在电影描写的同一时代也是个20多岁的纽约人，他认为这部剧犯了一些错误。

00:29:50.420 --> 00:29:54.280
<v David Rakoff>There are 525,600 minutes in a year.
<v David Rakoff>一年有525,600分钟。

00:29:54.280 --> 00:29:56.180
<v David Rakoff>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Rakoff>[笑声]

00:29:56.180 --> 00:29:58.930
<v David Rakoff>I learned that from watching Rent.
<v David Rakoff>我是从看《吉屋出租》里学到这个的。

00:29:58.930 --> 00:30:03.860
<v David Rakoff>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Rakoff>[笑声]

00:30:03.860 --> 00:30:37.900
<v David Rakoff>From watching Rent, I also learned that the best way to mark the passing of these 525,600 minutes would be to measure them out into something Jonathan Larson, the writer of the musical, called "seasons of love." What does that even mean, "seasons of love"? In Rent, the characters live out their seasons of love in huge lofts. Some of them have AIDS, which is coincidentally also the name of a dreaded global pandemic that is still raging and has killed millions of people worldwide. In Rent, however, AIDS seems to be a disease that renders one cuter and cuter.
<v David Rakoff>从看《吉屋出租》里，我还学到衡量这525,600分钟流逝的最佳方式，是将其划分为某种乔纳森·拉尔森（音乐剧作者）所谓的“爱之季节”。那到底是什么意思，“爱之季节”？在《吉屋出租》里，角色们生活在巨大的阁楼里，度过他们的爱之季节。其中一些人患有艾滋病，顺便说一句，这也是一种正在全球肆虐、夺走数百万生命的可怕全球流行病。然而在《吉屋出租》里，艾滋病似乎是一种让患者变得越来越可爱的疾病。

00:30:37.900 --> 00:30:41.680
<v David Rakoff>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Rakoff>[笑声]

00:30:41.680 --> 00:30:56.860
<v David Rakoff>The characters are artists, creative types. They have tatterdemalion clothes. Some of them are homosexual, and the ones who aren't homosexual don't even seem to mind. They screen their calls, and when it is their parents, they roll their eyes. They hate their parents.
<v David Rakoff>角色们都是艺术家、创意人士。他们穿着破烂的衣服。其中一些是同性恋，而那些不是同性恋的人似乎也完全不介意。他们筛选电话，当父母打来时，他们翻白眼。他们讨厌父母。

00:30:56.860 --> 00:30:58.000
<v David Rakoff>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Rakoff>[笑声]

00:30:58.000 --> 00:31:27.660
<v David Rakoff>They are never going back to Larchmont, no way. They will stay here, living in their 2,000 square feet of picturesque poverty, being sexually free and creative. Here are some ways to broadcast creativity in a movie. Start plinking out a tune on a piano. Scratch a few notes on some music paper. Plink some more. Suddenly crash both hands down on the keyboard. Then bring them quickly up to your head, and grab the hair at your temple, screaming, it won't work!
<v David Rakoff>他们永远不会回到拉奇蒙特，没门。他们会待在这里，住在他们2000平方英尺如画般的贫困中，保持性自由和创造力。以下是一些在电影中广播创造力的方式。在钢琴上开始叮叮当当地弹奏。在乐谱纸上潦草地写几个音符。再叮叮当当几下。突然把双手狠狠砸在琴键上。然后迅速将它们举到头边，抓住太阳穴的头发，尖叫道：它行不通！

00:31:27.660 --> 00:31:45.120
<v David Rakoff>Or sit at a typewriter, reading the page you've just written. Realize that it's shit, and tear it from the platen and toss it behind you. Cut to wastepaper basket overflowing with crumpled paper. Here's what they do in Rent to show that they are creative-- nothing! They do nothing!
<v David Rakoff>或者坐在打字机前，读着刚才写的那一页。意识到那是垃圾，把它从滚筒上撕下来扔到身后。切换到装满揉皱纸团的纸篓。这就是他们在《吉屋出租》中所做的展现他们富有创造力的方式——什么都不做！他们什么都不做！

00:31:45.120 --> 00:31:48.660
<v David Rakoff>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Rakoff>[笑声]

00:31:48.660 --> 00:32:06.680
<v David Rakoff>They hang out. And hanging out can be marvelous, but hanging out does not make you an artist. A secondhand wardrobe does not make you an artist. Neither do a hair-trigger temper, melancholic nature, propensity for tears, hating your parents, nor even HIV. I hate to say it. None of these can make you an artist.
<v David Rakoff>他们闲逛。闲逛可以是美妙的，但闲逛不会让你成为艺术家。二手衣橱不会让你成为艺术家。火爆的脾气、忧郁的性格、流泪的倾向、讨厌你的父母，甚至艾滋病毒都不会。我很遗憾这么说。这些都不能让你成为艺术家。

00:32:06.680 --> 00:32:07.740
<v David Rakoff>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Rakoff>[笑声]

00:32:07.740 --> 00:32:08.800
<v David Rakoff>They can help.
<v David Rakoff>它们可以帮上忙。

00:32:08.800 --> 00:32:10.180
<v David Rakoff>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Rakoff>[笑声]

00:32:10.180 --> 00:32:17.920
<v David Rakoff>But just as being gay does not make one witty, you can suck a mile of cock, it does not make you Oscar Wilde. Believe me, I know. I've tried.
<v David Rakoff>但正如身为同性恋并不会让人变得机智一样，你可以吸一英里长的鸡巴，但这不会让你成为奥斯卡·王尔德。相信我，我知道。我试过。

00:32:17.920 --> 00:32:19.140
<v David Rakoff>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Rakoff>[笑声]

00:32:19.140 --> 00:32:52.150
<v David Rakoff>The only thing that makes you an artist is making art. And that takes the opposite of hanging out. So when they sing the anthem of the show, that's a lie, really. Every song in the show is an anthem delivered with adolescent earnestness. It's like being trapped in the pages of a teenager's diary. So when they sing the title anthem of the show, "We're not going to pay this year's rent," followed by a kind of barked cheer of "rent, rent, rent, rent, rent, rent, rent," my only question is, well, why aren't you going to pay this year's rent?
<v David Rakoff>唯一能让你成为艺术家的是创作艺术。而那需要与闲逛完全相反的东西。所以当他们唱起剧中的赞美诗时，那其实是个谎言。剧中的每一首歌都是以一种青春期的恳切交付的赞美诗。就像被困在青少年的日记页里一样。所以当他们唱起剧中的同名赞美诗“我们今年不交房租”时，紧接着是一种近乎咆哮的欢呼“房租、房租、房租、房租、房租、房租、房租”，我唯一的问题是，好吧，为什么你们今年不交房租？

00:32:52.150 --> 00:32:53.150
<v David Rakoff>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Rakoff>[笑声]

00:32:53.150 --> 00:33:18.930
<v David Rakoff>It seems that they're not going to pay this year's rent because rent is for losers and uncreative types. Rent is for suits. By contrast, they are the last bastion of artistic purity. They have not sold out, and yet their brilliance goes unacknowledged. So fuck you, yuppie scum. I know what it's like to feel angry and ignored. I lived in Brooklyn a long time ago, about a block away from a prison.
<v David Rakoff>看起来他们今年不交房租，因为房租是失败者和缺乏创造力的人交的。房租是西装革履的人交的。相比之下，他们是艺术纯洁性的最后堡垒。他们没有出卖自己，然而他们的才华却得不到承认。去你的，雅痞人渣。我知道那种感到愤怒和被忽视的感觉。很久以前我住在布鲁克林，离监狱大约一个街区。

00:33:18.930 --> 00:33:20.230
<v David Rakoff>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Rakoff>[笑声]

00:33:20.230 --> 00:33:38.870
<v David Rakoff>During the day, the neighborhood bustled with lawyers, judges, criminals, bail bondsmen, private detectives. I lived on a block in a little two-story building that had once been a coach house in the 19th century, and the basement had a red dirt floor. On the ground floor below me was an office that did what exactly? Resumes? I can't remember.
<v David Rakoff>白天，那个社区挤满了律师、法官、罪犯、保释担保人和私人侦探。我住在一个小街区，是一栋在19世纪曾经是马车房的两层建筑，地下室是红土铺的地。在我楼下的底层是一个办公室，到底是做什么的？简历？我不记得了。

00:33:38.870 --> 00:34:03.290
<v David Rakoff>What I do remember is the man whose office it was. Raul was knee-bucklingly handsome. If my life had been different, like-- I don't know-- if I were like a hot girl with a driver's license, I could have put on a tube top and gone outside to wash my car in slow motion or something. But alas.
<v David Rakoff>我记得的是那个拥有办公室的人。劳尔长得帅得让人膝盖发软。如果我的生活不同，比如——我不知道——如果我是个有驾照的辣妹，我可以穿上管状背心去外面慢动作洗车之类的。但可惜啊。

00:34:03.290 --> 00:34:19.140
<v David Rakoff>Once, during the day-- it must have been the weekend because I was at home-- I could hear Raul having sex in the office downstairs. I skittered around my apartment like a cockroach on a frying pan, trying not to make any noise while desperately looking for a knothole in the crappy floorboards.
<v David Rakoff>有一次，在白天——那肯定是周末，因为我在家——我能听到劳尔在楼下的办公室做爱。我在我的公寓里像煎锅上的蟑螂一样乱窜，试图不发出任何声音，同时绝望地寻找烂地板上的结孔。

00:34:19.140 --> 00:34:20.450
<v David Rakoff>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Rakoff>[笑声]

00:34:20.450 --> 00:34:32.570
<v David Rakoff>Eventually, I just lay down flat against the tile of the kitchen floor, listening. Lying flat against the tile of my kitchen floor listening to someone else have sex is essentially my 20s in a nutshell.
<v David Rakoff>最后，我只是平躺在厨房的瓷砖地板上听着。平躺在厨房瓷砖地板上听别人做爱，本质上就是我20多岁的缩影。

00:34:32.570 --> 00:34:35.150
<v David Rakoff>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Rakoff>[笑声]

00:34:35.150 --> 00:34:58.290
<v David Rakoff>I was robbed in that neighborhood twice. And there were days when it hardly seemed worth it to live in a horrible part of town, just so that I could go daily to a stupid, soul-crushing, low-paying job, especially since as deeply as I yearned to be creative, for years and years, I was too scared to even try. So I did nothing. But here's something that I did do-- I paid my fucking rent.
<v David Rakoff>我在那个社区被抢劫过两次。有些日子，住在那种可怕的地方简直不值得，仅仅为了每天去那份愚蠢的、灵魂破碎的、低薪的工作，尤其是我多年来渴望成为创造者，却因为害怕而甚至不敢尝试。所以我什么都没做。但有一件事我做了——我交了我的混蛋房租。

00:34:58.290 --> 00:35:00.740
<v David Rakoff>[LAUGHTER]
<v David Rakoff>[笑声]

00:35:00.740 --> 00:35:05.630
<v David Rakoff>[APPLAUSE, CHEERS]
<v David Rakoff>[掌声，欢呼声]

00:35:05.630 --> 00:35:29.560
<v Ira Glass>The late David Rakoff. His essay about the musical Rent appears in his book Half Empty. But really, you can't go wrong with any of his books. Coming up, proof that some people simply should not go on vacation. They can't hack it. They cannot handle the mellow. That is in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>已故的大卫·拉科夫。他关于《吉屋出租》音乐剧的散文出现在他的书《半空》中。但真的，买他的任何书都不会错。接下来，证明有些人确实不该去度假。他们应付不来。他们无法驾驭那种悠闲。那一分钟后回来，来自芝加哥公共广播电台，我们的节目继续进行。

00:35:29.560 --> 00:35:54.040
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our show, of course, we choose a theme, bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, Got You Pegged-- stories of people making grand assumptions about other people, sometimes correct, sometimes deeply incorrect. Today's show is a rerun. We've arrived at Act 4 of our show. Act 4, Paradise Lost. Shalom Auslander has our story.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周我们都会选择一个主题，为你带来关于该主题的各种不同故事。今天的节目，“看透你了”——讲述人们对他人做出宏大假设的故事，有时是正确的，有时是极其错误的。今天的节目是重播。我们到达了节目的第四幕。第四幕，失乐园。沙洛姆·奥斯兰德带来了我们的故事。

00:35:54.040 --> 00:36:33.820
<v Shalom Auslander>I ruin vacations. That's just what I do. In Greece, I was sure the hotel had stuck us with the worst room in the building, even though every room was identical. I know that because we switched three times the day we got there. We switched again the morning after and went home two days early. A year later, I spent four days in a Jamaican rainforest complaining about the weather. We're in a rainforest, my wife said. So? So it rains. We went home two days early.
<v Shalom Auslander>我毁掉了度假。我就是这样的人。在希腊，我确信酒店给了我们楼里最差的房间，尽管每个房间都一模一样。我知道这一点，因为我们到那儿的第一天就换了三次房。第二天早上我们又换了一次，提前两天回家了。一年后，我在牙买加雨林待了四天，抱怨天气。我们正在雨林里，我妻子说。那又怎样？所以？所以它下雨了。我们提前两天回家了。

00:36:33.820 --> 00:36:56.460
<v Shalom Auslander>And so last year, when my wife informed me that she had booked us a six-day vacation in Anguilla, a remote island in the British West Indies, I decided that this time would be different. It was our first trip in a while, and I'd been doing a lot of work on myself. With the money I'd spent on therapy, I could have bought the whole damn island of Anguilla. So I was anxious to see how far I'd come.
<v Shalom Auslander>所以去年，当妻子告诉我她帮我们在安圭拉（英属西印度群岛的一个偏远岛屿）预订了六天假期时，我决定这次会不同。这是我们很久以来的第一次旅行，我一直在做自我提升工作。花在治疗上的钱，我都够买下整个安圭拉岛了。所以我很急切地想看看我进步了多少。

00:36:56.460 --> 00:37:18.060
<v Shalom Auslander>The resort was far more expensive than we could afford. I suspected that my wife was hoping that an exclusive resort would minimize the number of disappointments that could inevitably set me off. There was no need for her to worry. This time would be different. This time, I would be different.
<v Shalom Auslander>这个度假村的价格远超我们的承受能力。我怀疑我妻子是希望一个独家度假村能把不可避免会让我发火的失望事件减到最少。她不必担心。这次会不同。这次，我会变得不同。

00:37:18.060 --> 00:37:42.120
<v Shalom Auslander>The resort we flew to sits tucked away on the eastern end of an utterly pristine inlet of blue-green water on the southern tip of the island. The bellman brought our bags into our room, warned us not to feed the iguanas, wished us a happy stay, and left. My son started jumping on the bed. My wife joined him. And I pulled open the wooden louvered patio doors that led out to the beach.
<v Shalom Auslander>我们飞往的度假村坐落在岛屿南端一个完全原始的蓝绿色水域的东部一角，隐秘地藏在那里。行李员把我们的行李搬进房间，警告我们不要喂鬣蜥，祝我们入住愉快，然后离开了。我儿子开始在床上跳。我妻子加入了他的行列。我拉开了通往海滩的木制百叶窗露台门。

00:37:42.120 --> 00:38:13.800
<v Shalom Auslander>Wow, I said, as I stepped out onto the patio. For once, the view looked exactly as it had on the website. Pelicans circled above the calm, still waters. The owner's golden retrievers slept peacefully in the shade beneath a gentle, spreading palm. My wife came up behind me and put her arms around my waist. It's Eden, she said. Paradise, I answered. Not a cloud in the sky, she said. Not a single cloud, I said.
<v Shalom Auslander>“哇，”我走上露台时说道。这景色看起来和网站上一模一样。鹈鹕在平静的水面上盘旋。业主的金毛猎犬在舒展的棕榈树荫下安详地睡着。妻子走过来，搂住我的腰。“这是伊甸园，”她说。“天堂，”我回答。“天空没云，”她说。“没一朵云，”我说。

00:38:13.800 --> 00:38:42.650
<v Shalom Auslander>And then she went inside. When I came in, she already had her beach bag packed and was pulling a T-shirt over our son's head. We're going to the beach, she said. You coming? I'll come down after I unpack, I said. They hurried out the door, dropped a cracker for the iguana waiting in the shade at the bottom of the steps, and headed down to the beach. I quickly put away our clothing, grabbed the room key, and locked the patio door as I closed it behind me.
<v Shalom Auslander>然后她进屋了。等我进去时，她已经装好了沙滩包，正把T恤套在我儿子的头上。“我们要去海滩，”她说。“你要来吗？”“我整理完行李就下去，”我说。他们匆匆跑出门，给等在台阶底部阴影里的鬣蜥丢了一块饼干，然后朝海滩走去。我快速把衣服收好，拿上房门钥匙，关上露台门并将其锁上。

00:38:42.650 --> 00:39:17.430
<v Shalom Auslander>Hello, said the old man. He was standing at the foot of our patio, holding on to the rail for support. Oh, hello, I said. His name was Marvin, and he'd been coming to the resort for 25 years. Oh, 25 years at least, he said. Maybe more. Such a long time ago. Let's see, I'm 81 now. Yeah, the first time I came here was in, oh, let's see, 1980, I believe. Really? I said, backing my way towards the beach.
<v Shalom Auslander>“你好，”老男人说。他站在我们露台的脚下，扶着栏杆支撑身体。“哦，你好，”我说。他叫马文，已经在度假村住了25年了。“哦，至少25年了，”他说。“可能更久。很久以前了。看看，我现在81岁了。是的，我第一次来这儿是在……哦，看看，1980年，我想。真的吗？”我说着，退向海滩。

00:39:17.430 --> 00:39:41.150
<v Shalom Auslander>Here's the thing about people. I don't really like them. That's why I find racism so curious. There are so many reasons to dislike people. You're going to go with color? So I avoid the people whenever possible, try to keep my distance. It's really better for everyone.
<v Shalom Auslander>关于人，我不太喜欢他们。这就是为什么我觉得种族歧视如此奇怪。讨厌人的理由太多了。你偏偏要选肤色？所以我尽可能避开人，尽量保持距离。这对每个人都好。

00:39:41.150 --> 00:40:07.670
<v Shalom Auslander>Could have been 1982, Marvin continued, or maybe even 1978, now that I think about it. No, no, it was 1980. I remember because I was arguing with my wife about Reagan. Whew, boy, did she hate him. Uh-huh, I said. I looked toward the beach and could see my wife and son running down to the water with his pail and shovel. Well, I better go. Family's waiting for me and all.
<v Shalom Auslander>“可能是1982年，”马文继续说，“或者现在想来甚至可能是1978年。不，不，是1980年。我记得是因为我和妻子在争论里根。呼，天哪，她多讨厌他啊。”“嗯，”我说。我看向海滩，看到妻子和儿子正带着桶和铲子跑到水边。“好吧，我得走了。家人在等我之类的。”

00:40:07.670 --> 00:40:43.970
<v Shalom Auslander>She's dead now, he said. He gently cleared his throat and looked to the ground. Damn it, I thought. I'm sorry, I said. She was a good woman, he said. I nodded. I'm sure, I said. Hotel was very different back then, of course, Marvin continued. Very, very different. The restaurant wasn't where it is now, no, sir. That building wasn't even built until 1990 or so, maybe '92 even. Fellow named Jeremiah built it with his own two hands, by gosh. Nobody builds like that anymore.
<v Shalom Auslander>“她现在去世了，”他说。他轻轻清了清嗓子，看向地面。“见鬼，”我想。我表示抱歉，“她是个好女人，”他说。我点点头，“我肯定，”我说。“当时的酒店和现在很不一样，当然，”马文继续说。“非常，非常不同。餐厅不是现在这个位置，不是的。那座建筑直到1990年左右，甚至可能是92年才建起来。一个叫杰里迈亚的人亲手建造的，天哪。现在没人那样盖房子了。”

00:40:43.970 --> 00:41:05.210
<v Shalom Auslander>As Marvin rambled on, I began to wonder, was there any way I could switch rooms without upsetting my wife? But I stopped myself, pulled myself together. Not this time, I thought. We are having a pleasant vacation.
<v Shalom Auslander>随着马文的唠叨，我开始想，有没有什么办法能在不惹毛妻子的前提下换房间？但我克制住了，振作起来。这次不行，我想。我们正在享受愉快的假期。

00:41:05.210 --> 00:41:32.440
<v Shalom Auslander>Is that your wife? Marvin asked. Yeah, I said, waiting for me. She's beautiful, said Marvin. Yeah, I said. Well, I better skedaddle. My wife was beautiful, too. Uh-huh, I said, with a wave. Well, I'll see you later. Marvin waved back, walked up the steps of the villa directly adjoining ours, took out his key, and walked inside. The door closed, and the expectorating began.
<v Shalom Auslander>“那是你妻子吗？”马文问。“是的，”我说，“在等我。”“她很美，”马文说。“是的，”我说，“好吧，我得溜了。我的妻子也很美。”“嗯，”我说着挥了挥手。“好吧，回头见。”马文挥手回应，走上直接连接我们别墅的台阶，拿出钥匙，走了进去。门关上了，吐痰声开始了。

00:41:32.440 --> 00:41:34.830
<v Shalom Auslander>[COUGHING]
<v Shalom Auslander>[咳嗽声]

00:41:34.830 --> 00:41:37.710
