<v Shalom Auslander>[清嗓子，吐痰声]

00:41:37.710 --> 00:41:41.540
<v Shalom Auslander>[COUGHING]
<v Shalom Auslander>[咳嗽声]

00:41:41.540 --> 00:42:17.000
<v Shalom Auslander>Damn it, I thought. The trip had exhausted our son, so we had an early dinner and went for a short sunset stroll on the beach. Thanks, said my wife, putting her arm through mine. For what? I asked. For being so good about things. I smiled and squeezed her arm. Our son ran through the gentle surf and shrieked with joy. Soon he grew tired, climbed into my wife's arms, and we reluctantly climbed the patio steps back to the room.
<v Shalom Auslander>“该死，”我想。这次旅行耗尽了我儿子的体力，所以我们早早吃了晚饭，在海滩上进行了短暂的日落漫步。“谢谢，”妻子说，挽起我的胳膊。“为了什么？”我问。“为了你在这些事上表现得这么好。”我微笑并捏了捏她的胳膊。儿子跑过轻柔的浪花，高兴地尖叫。不久他累了，爬进妻子的怀里，我们不情愿地爬上露台台阶回到房间。

00:42:17.000 --> 00:42:44.520
<v Shalom Auslander>Good night, said Marvin. He was sitting on his patio next door. Good night, whispered my wife. Where in New York are you from? Asked Marvin. Upstate, she whispered, as she carried our exhausted son into the room. I spent a lot of time there, said Marvin. Hudson Valley, I think it was. Might have been the Catskills. Yeah, I think it was the Catskills. My wife and I used to take our kids up there when they were younger.
<v Shalom Auslander>“晚安，”马文说。他正坐在隔壁的露台上。“晚安，”妻子低声说。“你们是纽约哪儿的？”马文问。“北部，”她低声说着，把疲惫的儿子抱进房间。我 spent “我在那里待了很长时间，”马文说。“哈德逊河谷，我想是。可能是卡茨基尔。是的，我想是卡茨基尔。我和妻子年轻时常带孩子们去那儿。”

00:42:44.520 --> 00:43:23.810
<v Shalom Auslander>OK, I whispered. We don't talk much anymore-- my kids, I mean. Not my wife. She's dead. Sure, I whispered. Well, good night. I closed the door behind me and locked it. Jesus Christ, I said. You OK? Asked my wife. I'm fine, I said with a smile. We put our son to bed, crept quietly out the back door, and lay down together on a lounge chair underneath the stars. I love you, said my wife. I love you, too, I answered.
<v Shalom Auslander>“好的，”我低声说，“我们不再怎么说话了——我是说我的孩子们。不是我妻子。她去世了。”“当然，”我低声说，“好吧，晚安。”我关上门并锁好。“耶稣基督，”我说。“你还好吗？”妻子问。“我很好，”我微笑着说。我们把儿子安顿好，静静地爬出门，躺在一起看星星下的躺椅。“我爱你，”妻子说。“我也爱你，”我回答。

