00:43:55.180 --> 00:44:16.670
<v Shalom Auslander>I awoke the following morning in a dark mood. I didn't want my wife to see it, so I crept out of bed, quietly dressed, and went for a walk. I made my way to the open-air restaurant that overlooked the sea and sat down for an early breakfast. OK, I thought. So there's an old man next door. Was that worth ruining our whole vacation?
<v Shalom Auslander>第二天早上，我在阴郁的心情中醒来。我不希望妻子看到，于是悄悄起床，穿好衣服，出去散步。我走到海边的露天餐厅，坐下吃早饭。好吧，我想。隔壁有个老头。这值得毁了我们整个假期吗？

00:44:16.670 --> 00:44:38.750
<v Shalom Auslander>I'd worked too long and too hard on myself to be derailed by this. I decided to view Marvin as some sort of a test. Maybe it was God's test. Maybe it was just fate. But I was stronger than Marvin. I was stronger than a thousand Marvins. He would not defeat me. I finished my breakfast and walked back to the room.
<v Shalom Auslander>我在自我提升上花了太长时间、太多的努力，不能被这件事破坏。我决定把马文看作某种考验。也许这是上帝的考验。也许只是宿命。但我比马文坚强。我比一千个马文还要坚强。他打败不了我。我吃完早饭，走回房间。

00:44:38.750 --> 00:45:20.970
<v Shalom Auslander>Good morning, said Marvin. He was sitting at his patio table, finishing his breakfast. Good morning, I said. A lovely day, isn't it? Oh, it is, he said. Mornings like this, my wife and I used to get up early and go for a long swim. That sounds nice, I said. She's dead now, he said. Hey, I said, who isn't? That's true, said Marvin. I looked out over the beach and smiled. Check and mate, Marv. I was unflappable.
<v Shalom Auslander>“早安，”马文说。他正坐在露台桌旁吃早饭。“早安，”我说，“天气真好，不是吗？”“哦，是的，”他说，“像这样的早晨，我和妻子常起得很早去游泳。”“听起来不错，”我说。“她现在去世了，”他说。“嘿，”我说，“谁不是呢？”“那是真的，”马文说。我看着海滩，微笑。将死，马文。我处变不惊。

00:45:20.970 --> 00:45:45.250
<v Shalom Auslander>Auschwitz, said Marvin. I turned around. Sorry? My wife, she died in Auschwitz. We were quite young, only married a few years. Auschwitz, I said. Marvin nodded. We were sent to Dachau at first, but after a few weeks, they sent us to Auschwitz. Auschwitz, I said.
<v Shalom Auslander>“奥斯威辛，”马文说。我转过身。“对不起？”“我的妻子，她死在奥斯威辛。我们很年轻，才结婚几年。”“奥斯威辛，”我说。马文点点头。“我们最初被送到达豪，但几周后，他们把我们送到奥斯威辛。”“奥斯威辛，”我说。

00:45:45.250 --> 00:46:05.930
<v Shalom Auslander>Sure, my mother, too. She was sent to Bergen-Belsen. But that's not where she died. She died in Auschwitz with my father, probably of pneumonia. That's what I heard. My father was shot. The Nazis shot him. I know this from some people in Miami who knew him in the camps. Ethel, Morris Goldstein. They died a while ago.
<v Shalom Auslander>“当然，我母亲也是。她被送到卑尔根-贝尔森。但她不是在那儿死的。她和父亲一起死在奥斯威辛，大概是死于肺炎。这是我听说的。我父亲被枪杀了。纳粹枪杀了他。我是从迈阿密的一些在集中营认识他们的人那儿听说的。埃塞尔、莫里斯·戈尔茨坦。他们很久以前就去世了。”

00:46:05.930 --> 00:46:47.510
<v Shalom Auslander>I should have been compassionate. I know. I should have taken a pad and pen and committed his story to paper for future generations. But I didn't. Instead, I seethed. 20 minutes of genocide stories later, I went into our villa, closed the door sharply behind me, and stood in the center of the room with my hands on my hips. What? Asked my wife. I threw my hands into the air. Auschwitz, I said. Pardon? Auschwitz. What are you talking about? She asked. Au-freaking-schwitz. He's a survivor, hon, a Holocaust survivor.
<v Shalom Auslander>我本该表现出同情心的。我知道。我本该拿出纸笔，把他的故事写下来留给后代。但我没有。相反，我怒火中烧。20分钟的种族灭绝故事后，我走进别墅，猛地关上门，双手叉腰站在房间中央。“什么？”妻子问。我双手高举。“奥斯威辛，”我说。“什么？”“奥斯威辛。你在说什么？”她问。“奥——该死的——斯威辛。他是幸存者，亲爱的，大屠杀幸存者。”

00:46:47.510 --> 00:47:16.320
<v Shalom Auslander>I don't have anything against Holocaust survivors. Some of my best friends are Holocaust survivors. OK, that's not actually true, but I don't have anything against them. But if I want to relax and forget about life for a while, maybe hit a bar and have a drink, I'm not going to call Elie Wiesel. Hey, Elie. How's it going? I had a tough day. Why don't you come over, and we'll watch Schindler's List? Bring beer.
<v Shalom Auslander>我对大屠杀幸存者没什么意见。我最好的朋友中有些是大屠杀幸存者。好吧，那不是真的，但我对他们没什么反感。但如果我想放松一下，忘掉生活一段时间，也许去酒吧喝一杯，我是不会找埃利·威塞尔的。“嘿，埃利。最近怎么样？我过得很艰难。为什么不过来，我们一起看《辛德勒的名单》？带上啤酒。”

00:47:16.320 --> 00:47:39.640
<v Shalom Auslander>A Holocaust survivor, I said, pacing back and forth across the room. The place is half-empty, and the guy next door is a Holocaust survivor. I think I've been pretty good about this. I didn't let the travel upset me. I didn't let the hacking next door all night long get to me. But this is too much. It's too much. I'm standing in paradise, talking about gas chambers.
<v Shalom Auslander>“一个大屠杀幸存者，”我焦躁地在房间里走来走去，“酒店半空着，隔壁那家伙是个大屠杀幸存者。我觉得我已经表现得很好了。我没让旅行扫兴。我没让整夜的咳痰声影响我。但这太过分了。这太多了。我站在天堂，谈论毒气室。”

00:47:39.640 --> 00:48:03.960
<v Shalom Auslander>My wife was sympathetic, but she'd seen this before and insisted I was blowing it out of proportion. As usual, she said. And I said, What's that supposed to mean? And she said, You know what that means. And I said, No, I don't. And less than 24 hours after our plane touched down in Eden, we were fighting.
<v Shalom Auslander>我妻子很同情，但她以前见过这种情况，坚持说我夸大其词。“一如既往，”她说。我说，“那是什么意思？”她说，“你知道是什么意思。”我说，“不，我不知道。”在我们飞机降落在伊甸园不到24小时后，我们就吵架了。

00:48:03.960 --> 00:48:31.100
<v Shalom Auslander>It's your decision, she said to me, as she gathered her beach things together. He's not ruining our vacation. You are. No, I'm not! I shouted. Goebbels is. Blame Goebbels. She walked out and stomped down the patio steps. My son began to cry. A dozen iguanas sat on the deck. Two of them ran inside and hid under the bed. Damn it, I thought.
<v Shalom Auslander>“这是你的决定，”她说着，一边收拾沙滩用品，“他没毁掉我们的假期。是你。”“不，我没有！”我大喊，“是戈培尔。去怪戈培尔。”她走了出去，重重地走下露台台阶。我儿子开始哭。十几只鬣蜥坐在露台上。两只跑进屋躲在床下。“该死，”我想。

00:48:31.100 --> 00:48:49.020
<v Shalom Auslander>I spent the next few hours avoiding my wife, hoping that a little time would settle things down between us. I took my son and walked over to the front office. Did you feed them? The man asked. We told you not to feed them. They're in our room, I said. They're under our bed.
<v Shalom Auslander>接下来的几个小时我都在躲着妻子，希望时间能平息我们之间的矛盾。我带着儿子走到办公室。“你们喂它们了吗？”男人问，“我们告诉过你们不要喂它们。它们在我们的房间里，”我说，“它们在我们的床下。”

00:48:49.020 --> 00:49:12.280
<v Shalom Auslander>I heard laughter coming from the lobby and poked my head around the corner. Marvin was sitting in a wicker chair, surrounded by a half a dozen adoring hotel employees. A waiter from the bar brought him a rum punch. Let me pay you, said Marvin. No, no, Mr. Marvin, said the waiter. No, no. If there's anything worse than hating someone, it's discovering that everyone else loves them.
<v Shalom Auslander>我听到大堂传来笑声，探头一看。马文坐在藤椅上，被半打崇拜的酒店员工包围着。酒吧里的服务员给他拿了一杯朗姆潘趣酒。“让我付钱，”马文说。“不，不，马文先生，”服务员说，“不，不。”如果说有什么比讨厌一个人更糟糕的事，那就是发现其他人竟然都爱他。

00:49:12.280 --> 00:49:34.320
<v Shalom Auslander>Oh, Mr. Marvin, an attractive female chambermaid cried. He's terrible! The man at the front desk smiled in Marvin's direction. What a sweet man, he said, and then shook his head. And what he's been through.
<v Shalom Auslander>“哦，马文先生，”一位迷人的女房务员尖叫道，“他太棒了！”前台的男人朝着马文微笑。“多可爱的人啊，”他说，然后摇了摇头，“他经历过什么啊。”

00:49:34.320 --> 00:50:12.810
<v Shalom Auslander>At dinner that evening, Marvin sat at the resort owner's table. My wife and I sat nearby, barely talking. Marvin told funny stories, and everyone laughed. Marvin told sad stories about the Holocaust, I guessed, judging by the horror on the face of his dinner companions, and everyone hugged him. And when the bill came and Marvin reached for it, again, they refused to let him pay. That was when I decided with absolute certainty that the son-of-a-bitch was faking it.
<v Shalom Auslander>那天晚餐时，马文坐在度假村业主的桌旁。我和妻子坐在附近，几乎没说话。马文讲着有趣的故事，大家都笑了。马文讲着关于大屠杀的悲伤故事（我想，看他餐伴脸上恐惧的表情），大家都拥抱他。当账单来了，马文伸手去拿时，他们又拒绝让他付钱。就在那一刻，我极其确定那个混蛋是装的。

00:50:12.810 --> 00:50:31.810
<v Shalom Auslander>Holocaust survivor, my ass, I thought. I grew up in an Orthodox Jewish community. I've known Holocaust survivors, OK? And they don't go on and on about it. Elie Wiesel doesn't go on about it. Marvin was no Holocaust survivor. He was just an old chatterbox looking for a meal ticket.
<v Shalom Auslander>大屠杀幸存者，我才不信，我想。我在一个正统犹太社区长大。我知道大屠杀幸存者，好吗？他们不会没完没了地讲那件事。埃利·威塞尔也不会没完没了地讲。马文根本不是什么大屠杀幸存者。他只是一个找饭票的老唠叨鬼。

00:50:31.810 --> 00:50:50.330
<v Shalom Auslander>There's no such thing as a free lunch until you tell everyone sitting next to you at lunch about your stay in Bergen-Belsen. Marvin was faking, I was sure of it. And I was equally sure that if I could just prove that to my wife, all would be forgiven. And I knew just how to do it.
<v Shalom Auslander>没有免费的午餐，直到你告诉午餐时坐在你旁边的每个人关于你在卑尔根-贝尔森的经历。马文是装的，我对此深信不疑。我也同样确定，如果我能证明这一点给我妻子看，一切都会被原谅。而且我知道该怎么做。

00:50:50.330 --> 00:51:09.830
<v Shalom Auslander>We came back from dinner, and I put my son into his pajamas and read him some stories. My wife sang him some songs and put him to bed. And after that, she put on her shoes and said she needed to take a walk. Fine, I said. Good, she answered. Great, I said. Whatever, she replied.
<v Shalom Auslander>我们晚餐回来，我让儿子换上睡衣，给他讲了些故事。妻子唱了些歌让他入睡。之后，她穿上鞋说她需要去散步。“行，”我说。“好，”她回答。“太棒了，”我回答。“随便你，”她回答。

00:51:09.830 --> 00:51:30.030
<v Shalom Auslander>I went outside, sat down on the patio steps, and watched the ocean, hoping it would calm me down. Suddenly, I heard a groan coming from Marvin's patio. I stood up, peeked over the fence that separated our two patios, and saw him asleep on one of his lounge chairs. This was my chance.
<v Shalom Auslander>我走到外面，坐在露台台阶上，看着大海，希望它能让我冷静下来。突然，我听到马文的露台传来一声呻吟。我站起来，窥视分隔我们两个露台的栅栏，看到他在他的躺椅上睡着了。这是我的机会。

00:51:30.030 --> 00:51:57.790
<v Shalom Auslander>I looked around to see if anyone was watching, went over to his patio, and crept up the stairs. He rustled, and I froze. After a few seconds, I tiptoed over to where he was sleeping. His left arm was propped behind his head, but his right arm was stretched out along the arm of the chair. Bingo. I made my way back to our room and waited anxiously for my wife to return.
<v Shalom Auslander>我环顾四周看看有没有人看，走到他的露台，轻手轻脚地爬上台阶。他动了一下，我僵住了。几秒钟后，我踮着脚尖走到他睡觉的地方。他的左臂撑在脑后，但右臂沿着椅子的扶手伸出来。宾果。我回到了我们的房间，焦急地等待妻子的归来。

00:51:57.790 --> 00:52:36.810
<v Shalom Auslander>A few minutes later, the front door opened. Well, well, well, I said, as she came into the room. Well, well what? She asked. Well, well, well. I guess Mr. Auschwitz isn't such a survivor after all. What are you talking about? She asked. I held my arm out and pointed to my forearm. No numbers, I said with a smile. At last, I'd gotten a clean look at his forearm, and no numbers. What? Asked my wife. No numbers. If he was in the Holocaust, where are the numbers?
<v Shalom Auslander>几分钟后，前门开了。“好，好，好，”她进房间时我说。“好什么？”她问。“好，好，好。我想奥斯威辛先生并不是什么幸存者。”“你在说什么？”她问。我伸出胳膊，指着我的前臂。“没有数字，”我笑着说。终于，我清晰地看到了他的前臂，没有数字。“什么？”妻子问。“没有数字。如果他经历了大屠杀，数字在哪儿？”

00:52:36.810 --> 00:52:57.570
<v Shalom Auslander>Her face dropped. What did you do? She asked. What? What did you do? I didn't do anything. Did you ask him to show you his numbers? Bloody hell, Shal. Did you ask him to show you his numbers? I didn't ask him to show me his numbers, I said. He was sleeping.
<v Shalom Auslander>她的脸垮了下来。“你做了什么？”她问。“什么？你做了什么？”“我什么都没做。”“你让他给你看数字了？该死的，沙尔。你让他给你看数字了？”“我没让他给我看数字，”我说，“他在睡觉。”

00:52:57.570 --> 00:53:33.460
<v Shalom Auslander>She pressed her fingertips against her eyes and shook her head. And why exactly would he lie about being in the Holocaust? Why? I asked. Free stuff. They pay for all his meals. They probably pay for his room. He's faking it. This is ludicrous. Why would he pick the Holocaust? What's he going to say, Bataan Death March? It was a hike. Get over it. You want free stuff, you go with Holocaust.
<v Shalom Auslander>她用指尖按住眼睛，摇了摇头。“他为什么要谎称自己经历了大屠杀？为什么？”我问。“免费东西。他们付了他所有的饭钱。他们可能还付了他的房钱。他是装的。这太荒谬了。他为什么选大屠杀？他要说什么，巴丹死亡行军？那是一次徒步旅行。算了吧。想要免费东西，你就选大屠杀。”

00:53:33.460 --> 00:54:13.240
<v Shalom Auslander>Which arm? She asked. What? Which arm did you check? I paused. The right one, I said. She looked at me. They tattooed the left? She nodded. And they didn't tattoo everyone. How do you know? My neighbors were survivors, she said, both neighbors. None of them had numbers. She kicked her shoes off and headed for the bathroom. This isn't about Marvin, she said. This isn't about numbers or concentration camps. This is about you.
<v Shalom Auslander>“哪只胳膊？”她问。“什么？你检查了哪只胳膊？”我停顿了一下，“右边，”我说。她看着我，“他们刺在左边？”她点点头，“而且他们不是每个人都刺。你怎么知道？”“我的邻居是幸存者，”她说，“两个邻居都是。他们都没有数字。”她踢掉鞋子，朝浴室走去，“这不是马文的事，”她说，“这不是关于数字或集中营的事。这是关于你的事。”

00:54:13.240 --> 00:54:32.520
<v Shalom Auslander>All that night and into the next morning, I couldn't sleep. I lay in bed, imagining that I'd proved to everyone what a fraud Marvin was. I pictured finding a photo of him circa 1940, somewhere in Miami. I pictured calling the Holocaust Museum in DC. Marvin? They would say. We have no record of a Marvin.
<v Shalom Auslander>那天晚上一直到第二天早上，我都睡不着。我躺在床上，想象着我向所有人证明了马文是个骗子。我设想找到一张他在1940年左右在迈阿密的照片。我设想给华盛顿的大屠杀博物馆打电话。“马文？”他们会说，“我们没有任何关于马文的记录。”

00:54:32.520 --> 00:55:06.900
<v Shalom Auslander>I pictured confronting him at dinner, catching him out on some esoteric German historical fact. Wrong. Himmler didn't take over the Gestapo until 1934. Ha! But mostly, I just felt awful. A full third of our first family vacation was over, and we'd spent most of it fighting. I was sure our son sensed it, sure that he would hold it against me. Sure I was wrong, but sure I was right.
<v Shalom Auslander>我设想在晚餐时面对他，用深奥的德国历史事实戳穿他。错。希姆莱直到1934年才接管盖世太保。哈！但大部分时间，我只是感到糟糕透顶。我们第一次家庭度假的三分之一已经过去了，我们大部分时间都在吵架。我确信我儿子感觉得到，确信他会因此记恨我。确信我错了，但确信我是对的。

00:55:06.900 --> 00:55:38.020
<v Shalom Auslander>At the first sign of daybreak, I got out of bed and went down to the beach. I walked south along the entire length of the shore before turning around and heading back. As I grew closer to the beach across from our villa, I saw something dark and heavy in the shallow water. I thought it was a log or a mass of seaweed, but then I saw it was moving. It was kicking. It was a person. And as I grew closer still, I realized it was Marvin. It was Marvin, and he was struggling to get to shore.
<v Shalom Auslander>破晓时分，我起床去海滩。我沿着海岸线往南走了一整段，然后掉头往回。当我走近我们别墅对面的海滩时，我看到浅水里有一个又黑又重的东西。我以为那是根木头或一团海藻，但随后我看到它在动。它在踢。是个人。当我走得更近时，我意识到是马文。是马文，他正在挣扎着上岸。

00:55:38.020 --> 00:56:03.510
<v Shalom Auslander>I hate to admit it, but even as I ran to help him, I was pissed off. It was bad enough I had to put up with this pain in the ass. Now I had to save him? I grabbed Marvin around his chest, lifting and pulling as I swung his arm across my shoulder. He was coughing, spluttering, trying to catch his breath. I'm all right, he was saying. I'm all right. I helped him onto the beach. I was breathing heavily.
<v Shalom Auslander>我很不情愿承认，但即使在我跑去救他时，我也火大。我忍受这个讨厌鬼已经够糟了。现在我还得救他？我抓住马文的胸口，把他提起，把他的胳膊架在我的肩膀上。他在咳嗽，吐着唾沫，试图吸气。“我没事，”他说，“我没事。”我把他扶上岸。我气喘吁吁。

00:56:03.510 --> 00:56:42.750
<v Shalom Auslander>That, I said, was the most pathetic cry for attention I have ever seen. Marvin laughed and took a moment to catch his breath. My wife, he said. Yeah, I said. Auschwitz. He shook his head. Second wife, he said. We came here together for 25 years. We used to get up for early morning swims. As I got older, it got harder for me to get back to the shore-- undertow and all. She would stand at the edge and wait for me to come back. She sounds great, I said. She was, said Marvin.
<v Shalom Auslander>“那，”我说，“是我见过最可怜的求关注方式。”马文笑了笑，花了一会儿平复呼吸。“我的妻子，”他说。“是啊，”我说，“奥斯威辛。”他摇摇头，“第二任妻子，”他说，“我们一起在那儿住了25年。我们常起来早泳。我老了以后，很难再回到岸边——回流什么的。她会站在边缘等我回来。”“她听起来很棒，”我说。“她是，”马文说。

00:56:42.750 --> 00:57:05.770
<v Shalom Auslander>I shook my head. Such a shame she wasted all those years with you. Marvin laughed again. We have the same sense of humor, he said. We should spend more time together. I stood up, wiped the sand from my legs and hands, and uttered the most honest thing I'd said to Marvin since I met him. That sounds awful.
<v Shalom Auslander>我摇摇头，“真可惜她和你浪费了那么多年。”马文又笑了，“我们有同样的幽默感，”他说，“我们应该多待在一起。”我站起来，擦掉腿和手上的沙子，说了我从见到马文以来最真诚的一句话：“那听起来太糟糕了。”

00:57:05.770 --> 00:57:24.010
<v Shalom Auslander>Marvin smiled, and we walked back to our rooms. And after that moment, he never bothered me again. I'd wasted three days trying to be polite to someone I couldn't stand and nearly ruined another vacation. But by finally being the ass I really am, I'd saved it.
<v Shalom Auslander>马文笑了，我们走回房间。从那一刻起，他再没烦过我。我浪费了三天试图对一个我无法忍受的人礼貌，差点又毁了一个假期。但通过终于成为我本性中那个混蛋的样子，我拯救了它。

00:57:24.010 --> 00:57:42.890
<v Shalom Auslander>I was cordial enough. Marvin waved when I saw him in the TV room, telling Holocaust stories to the young couple who checked in that evening, and I waved back. He nodded when I saw him at breakfast the following morning, telling a pair of young waitresses about mass graves in Sobibor, and I nodded back.
<v Shalom Auslander>我足够客气了。我在电视室看到马文时会招手，他正给那天刚入住的年轻夫妇讲大屠杀故事，我也招手回应。第二天早上吃早饭看到他点头致意时，他正在给两个年轻女服务员讲索比堡的乱葬坑，我也点头回应。

00:57:42.890 --> 00:58:04.870
<v Shalom Auslander>And then we finished our breakfast. And my wife took my hand in hers, and she smiled at me, and I smiled at her. And together, we walked arm in arm to watch the sun still rising over the unspoiled beach below.
<v Shalom Auslander>然后我们吃完了早饭。妻子握住我的手，对我微笑，我也对她微笑。我们手臂挽着手臂，看着太阳在下方未被污染的海滩上徐徐升起。

00:58:04.870 --> 00:58:09.880
<v Ira Glass>Shalom Auslander. He's the author most recently of the memoir Feh.
<v Ira Glass>沙洛姆·奥斯兰德。他最近的回忆录《Feh》的作者。

00:58:09.880 --> 00:58:50.550
<v Ira Glass>["BE CAREFUL OF THE STONES YOU THROW" BY SHIRLEY CAESAR]
<v Ira Glass>["小心你扔出的石头" - 雪莉·凯撒]

